From 357fe4781a7d417cfc4c9ae93c35f188953086a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 23:51:31 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Frame interpolation: instances of one mesh pair by where they
 are

Instances of one mesh (same vertices, indices, texture and count) paired
up in drawing order. When a copy at the front of the list leaves the view,
every copy after it paired with its neighbour. A neighbour farther than
the own-motion guard followed the camera, but one closer was kept and
blended halfway towards its neighbour, so the in-between frames drew it
where neither frame had it: flicker. Dissidia 012's World of Darkness is
a hall of copies (about 2,600 draws a frame); the owner saw its pillars
flicker at 60 and 90 fps and not at 30.

The camera estimate suffered too. Every copy paired with a neighbour turns
exactly as the camera does and slides by the distance between the two, so
the median turn could pick such a pair: in World of Darkness a 33-unit
"camera move" in one frame, cut as a camera jump (19 times in a minute of
battle).

Now the camera is the motion the most pairs of meshes drawn once agree on
(everything that stands still moves by the same matrix; rounding aside),
falling back to any pairs and then to the median turn. Then a copy whose
drawing-order partner is where that camera carries it keeps it, and the
rest of each mesh pair up nearest first, globally, so a copy that left the
view does not take its neighbour's place. Off switch:
<prefix>_INTERPOLATION_ORDER_PAIRS (drawing order and the median turn, as
before). The trace and the per-second guard line count the pairs taken out
of drawing order.

<prefix>_CHECK_REPLAY now also counts the halfway frame's pixels far from
both frames (a colour channel more than 40 off each), and =N sets its
interval in flips.

Verified on Windows with Dissidia 012 (clang release build, 60 fps,
internal scale 4, scripted Quick Battles): on a timeline that repeats run
after run, halfway pixels far from both frames went from 15.93/2.07/3.92/
0.73% to 11.96/1.37/3.13/0.64% at four checks, and the distant pillar that
drew halfway between two copies no longer does; camera cuts in a minute of
World of Darkness 19 -> 1. Old Chaos Shrine, same timeline: 1.401 vs
1.399% (no change). Story Mode world map (orb pickup): identical. Render
time within noise (5.20-5.46 ms before, 5.37-5.49 after). The framework's
21 tests pass, three of them new (swapped instances, a row that loses its
first copy, a hall of copies' camera). Not tested: the Steam Deck, macOS
and Linux builds, other games (their thresholds differ; the rule scales
with max_own_motion).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
